<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Timucua</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Timucua were a Native American people of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florida">Florida</a> and south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a>, divided into many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chiefdom">chiefdoms</a> that spoke dialects of the Timucua language. They met French <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huguenots">Huguenots</a> at Fort Caroline in 1564 and from the late 16th century were drawn into the Spanish Franciscan mission system. Epidemics, wars and slave raids by the English and their allies reduced them sharply, and they had largely disappeared by the late 18th century.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The missions were the other instrument of rule: the Jesuits began them in 1567 and withdrew in 1572, and from 1573 the Franciscans ran dozens among the Guale, <b>Timucua</b> and Apalachee.</blockquote><a href="/empire/spanish-florida">Spanish Florida · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>It depended on food from the <b>Timucua</b> chief Saturiwa and his neighbours, and hunger and mutiny troubled it.</blockquote><a href="/state/french-florida">French Florida</a></li><li><blockquote>Franciscans built a chain of missions among the <b>Timucua</b>, Guale and Apalachee, who supplied food and labour.</blockquote><a href="/state/spanish-florida">Spanish Florida</a></li></ul></section>
